-- THE "REMARKABLE EDIFICE" OF LEAN
-- The text says: "Everything except Universes and Arrows is an Inductive Type."
-- Let's enable "pp.all" (pretty print all) to see the truth behind the syntax.

-- 1. DATA (Obvious)
-- Nat is inductive (Zero, Succ)
#print Nat
-- inductive Nat : Type
-- | zero : Nat
-- | succ : Nat → Nat


-- 2. LOGIC (Surprising!)
-- "And" (∧) is just a Structure (which is a single-constructor Inductive Type).
-- It holds two proofs: one for 'a', one for 'b'.
#print And
-- inductive And : Prop → Prop → Prop
-- | intro : ∀ {a b : Prop}, a → b → And a b


-- 3. CHOICE (Or)
-- "Or" (∨) is an Inductive Type with TWO constructors.
-- You can build it using the Left path OR the Right path.
#print Or
-- inductive Or : Prop → Prop → Prop
-- | inl : ∀ {a b : Prop}, a → Or a b
-- | inr : ∀ {a b : Prop}, b → Or a b


-- 4. EQUALITY (=)
-- Even Equality is an Inductive Type!
-- It has only ONE constructor: `refl` (Reflexivity).
-- "a = b" is defined as "The family of types generated by a = a".
#print Eq
-- inductive Eq : α → α → Prop
-- | refl : ∀ (a : α), Eq a a


-- 5. FALSEHOOD (False)
-- How do we define "False"?
-- It is an Inductive Type reasoning: "If I have no constructors, I can never be created."
#print False
-- inductive False : Prop


-- CONCLUSION:
-- We didn't need to "hard code" Logic.
-- We built Logic out of Data Structures (Inductive Types).
-- And (Product), Or (Sum), Eq (Reflexivity), False (Empty).
